"""
patch_L291_13_L168_moon_mean_motion_20260909.py -- the Moon's orbit: L-168's source fix (gallery repo)

Built on gallery da57d0959620f7b6f0b86030b03299d5b8a89a8e
at https://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io (main).
Ledger: L-168 (the fix it asked for), L-291 (step 3, Mode 5 round 3).

Tony's round 3, 2026-09-09: the Moon's orbit drew as a lattice of long
chords, which he recognised from the orrery as points too far apart in
time. Measured here from the page's own driver against the live cache:
the trusted arc's 121 points swept 8,406 degrees -- 23 orbits -- over a
6.84-day window. Round 2's opacity change was aimed at the wrong thing;
this is the cause.

  gallery/assembler/render_orbits.py -- propagate_marker() derived mean
  motion from SOLAR gravity (K_GAUSS / a**1.5): right for a planet,
  wrong by three orders of magnitude for a moon about its planet. L-168
  recorded this in July with the fix design: use the served Horizons
  mean motion (n_deg_per_day, on every osculating block since M2). Done
  as designed. A block with no served n is refused with a plain message,
  never given solar GM quietly.

  Checked here against Horizons' own as_of_today positions in the live
  cache: Earth, Moon, Io, Titan all agree to a part in a billion. (Halley
  sits at 5e-3 with the old formula and the new, unrelated: its elements
  are pinned at the 1986 perihelion.) The Artifact 1 pin holds -- 5
  verdicts and T3's feature set unchanged. The Moon's arc now sweeps 96
  degrees, ~13.2 deg/day, and the marker sits on it.

  Also fixed by this: the Moon's MARKER. The page's epoch is 00:00 UTC
  and the nightly runs at ~17:40 UTC, so between them the elements were
  a day old and 132 rad/day put the Moon anywhere on its orbit.

  documentation/payload_earth_scene.json -- regenerated with the fixed
  propagator from the cache of 2026-09-09 (the old fixture carried the
  23-orbit arc). documentation/smoke_earth_geometry.js -- pins the arc's
  sweep at 60-120 degrees; the old arc fails it by a factor of 70.

HOW TO RUN: save to the GALLERY repo root, Run, then
gallery_maintenance_run.py (expect 6 of 6), commit, push, --live, and
tap the Moon: one thin grey ellipse, one bright white quarter-arc, the
Moon on it. Nothing to remove -- the chords were never meant to be there.

GUARDS: three files md5-checked (LF) against da57d095; each hunk matches
once; ASCII-only; refuses a second run; writes nothing on failure.

Written September 2026 with Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.
"""
